// src/v4/std/node.dag
//
// Scope: foundational substrate — type connectives + L1 behaviors. THE root of the epistemic chain.
// Anchor: THESIS.md "Substrate shape" §198-203 + MODELING.md
//
// Owns:
//   - 6 type connectives: Atom | Conj | Disj | Arrow | Cardinality | Instantiation
//   - 5 L1 behaviors: Value | Transform | Branch | Loop | Bind
//   - C1 stop-signal discipline (no 7th connective, no 6th behavior)
//   - Symbol: opaque kernel identity (K-1, operator-ratified 2026-05-15)
//   - Node / NodeKind / Edge: the one recursive type + its skeleton
//   - the edge-discipline structural invariant (a connective fixes its
//     edge shape; the illegal pairing is never silently constructible)
//
// A1 (operator-ratified 2026-05-15) — the recursion contract:
//   - ONE recursive type: Node. Recursion lives SOLELY in Node children
//     (the children list), never in a self-referential type definition.
//   - A Node carries ORTHOGONAL flat-discriminant coordinates — connective
//     (∈ the closed 6, incl. `Instantiation` which is how genericity is
//     expressed: parameter-names + the Instantiation connective, NOT a
//     recursive Generic type) and, for computation Nodes, behavior (∈ the
//     closed 5). The two-coordinated-substrate framing (THESIS §198-203)
//     = these axes stay orthogonal; it does NOT license a 2nd recursive
//     type. Behaviors are STRUCTURAL, never lens-derived (same discipline
//     as feedback_no_annotations); THESIS's uncommitted "unified substrate"
//     means *deriving behaviors away* — rejected here.
//   - A separate recursive Behavior / Inferred / Pattern / Generic type is
//     the bounded-kernel violation that split v2 infer into 12 files —
//     STOP and escalate, NOT a worker choice.
//   - Recursive generics: a recursive generic is a FINITE Node tree with
//     an `Instantiation` connective + a name-reference Node back to its
//     own declaration (resolved through the namespace — feedback_naming_is
//     _aliasing); it is "just Node underlying." Regular recursion (same
//     parameter at the recursive occurrence) is unconditionally admissible
//     and decidable. Non-regular / polymorphic recursion (recursive
//     occurrence at a different instantiation) is a STOP unless a decidable
//     bound is exhibited (defers to the A2 decidability resolution).
//
// A2 (operator-ratified 2026-05-15) — termination is Tier-1, not optional:
//   - `Loop` is BOUNDED RECURSION, total-by-construction (the
//     Coq/Agda/Idris totality choice — INVARIANTS:72). No Y-combinator,
//     no general recursion. This is the established INVARIANT P4 made
//     explicit as the T-1 substrate shape.
//   - Termination is a Tier-1 (compile-time) structural fact carried as
//     descent evidence: IMPLICIT when recursion descends on a sub-Node
//     (the bounded-kernel "recursion in children" default — the common
//     case, no measure needed); EXPLICIT RankingDimension/TerminationProof
//     (lexicographic/multiset per Dershowitz-Manna) when descent is not
//     structural.
//   - A CHECKER reading VERIFIES the descent proof (decidable). It never
//     searches/discovers a measure (INVARIANTS:66 "checker not discoverer"
//     = feedback_no_engine). `DescentUnknown` ⇒ fail-closed Diagnostic,
//     never a fabricated or assumed termination.
//   - The bound IS the structural datum lens/cost.dag reads — termination
//     ∧ complexity are ONE read on the U1 homomorphism-with-cost spine
//     (this is why C2 cost derivation is sound).
//   - Genuine unboundedness (servers/streams/REPLs) is a TERMINATING step
//     iterated by the coordination-boundary driver — never an unbounded
//     `.dag` loop.
//   - Residual boundary (ratified, not buried): a true-but-not-
//     structurally-rankable termination (open conjecture / un-encodable
//     math) is NOT expressible as a total value — express it as bounded
//     search returning a fail-closed typed result; you may not CLAIM a
//     totality you cannot prove. Where even that is impossible ⇒ STOP-
//     escalate (a modeling conversation, not a worker hack). Correct
//     closure of a decidable system (INVARIANTS:31), not a defect.
//
// K-1 (operator-ratified 2026-05-15) — kernel identifiers are opaque:
//   - `Symbol` is an opaque identity: equality-comparable and NOTHING
//     else. The type exposes no content accessor — kernel and lens code
//     physically cannot read or branch on an identifier's spelling.
//   - This forecloses the v2 failure mode structurally, not by review:
//     a readable kernel String became a heuristic-smuggling channel
//     (`match name { "x" => ... }`, prefix checks; INVARIANTS:229,
//     feedback_opaque_strings_attract_heuristics). With an opaque Symbol
//     the heuristic is UNWRITABLE — the type won't yield content
//     (feedback_no_textual_enforcement_bridges: structure, not a test).
//   - The human-readable spelling lives in a boundary intern table,
//     consulted ONLY at diagnostics rendering and emission — never by
//     kernel/lens logic. Whether Symbol is backed by a hash or an intern
//     index is hidden by the type (seed/runtime implementation detail).
//   - Bonus consistency: with opaque Symbol the content-hash fold over an
//     `Atom` is automatically rename-invariant — α-equivalent programs
//     hash equal — because the fold cannot observe the spelling.
//
// T-1 scope dispositions (operator-ratified 2026-05-15):
//   - Q1: `Atom` carries an opaque `Symbol` identity only. Literal VALUE
//     typing (int/string/bool literal data) is std/primitive.dag (T-3) —
//     a different concern from identifier identity.
//   - Q2: this file owns the `Loop` behavior variant + the A2 discipline
//     above; the descent-evidence carriers (RankingDimension /
//     TerminationProof / DescentEvidence) belong to std/cardinality.dag
//     (T-3, P4 decidability). The IMPLICIT descent case (recursion on a
//     proper sub-Node child) needs no carrier — it is free from A1's
//     children structure; only explicit non-structural ranking needs one.
//   - `inhabits` is NOT a field of `Node`: A1 forbids non-`children`
//     Node-recursion, and inhabitance is a cross-declaration relation
//     expressed via name-reference `Atom`s (resolved through the
//     namespace) — declared/owned by std/algebra.dag (T-2).
//
// Consumes:
//   nothing — this is the substrate root
//
// Status: T-1 modeled 2026-05-15 (cool-boar-275). The I/O contract in
// this header is immutable for downstream workers. Splitting this file
// requires explicit operator ratification (substrate extension = stop
// signal).


module v4.std.node


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Symbol — opaque kernel identity (K-1).
//
// Body-less by design: an opaque type yields no content. Equality is the
// only operation; there is deliberately no accessor that returns the
// spelling. Symbol is substrate-ambient (declared at the substrate root,
// like the kernel List/Int/Bool) — it is not "consumed" from another
// file. See header K-1 for the full rationale.
type Symbol


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Connective — the closed type-substrate classifier. Exactly six.
//
// `Atom` is terminal at the user-input boundary: it carries an opaque
// `Symbol` identity (an identifier / type-parameter name) and decomposes
// no further. The other five connectives are pure structural
// discriminants whose entire content is their `children` edges.
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). A seventh connective is an edit to THIS
// declaration — a visible diff routed to operator ratification (per
// STRUCTURE.md A3: the substrate does not magically refuse it; the edit
// is loud and un-missable). There is no `Other` / open-ended variant.
type Connective
  = Atom { identity: Symbol }
  | Conj
  | Disj
  | Arrow
  | Cardinality
  | Instantiation


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Behavior — the closed computation-substrate classifier. Exactly five.
//
// Flat unit variants: a behavior carries no payload. All structure (a
// Loop's body, a Branch's paths, a Transform's operands) lives in the
// owning Node's `children`. This file owns the five-behavior ENUM and
// the A2 `Loop`-is-bounded discipline; per-behavior edge wiring is built
// by the pipeline-stage tasks (T-6+).
//
// CLOSED (C1 stop-signal). A sixth behavior is an edit to THIS
// declaration — a visible diff routed to operator ratification.
type Behavior
  = Value
  | Transform
  | Branch
  | Loop
  | Bind


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// NodeKind — which of the two coordinated substrates a Node lives in.
//
// Shape S (operator-confirmed 2026-05-15): a Node belongs to EXACTLY one
// substrate. A type node has a connective; a computation node has a
// behavior. It is NOT the case that one node carries both — that would
// conflate the two independent C1 closures. The connective and behavior
// enums stay orthogonal closed sets; `NodeKind` merely selects which
// one applies (THESIS:198-203 — computation references type by edge, it
// does not co-locate the two coordinates on one node).
type NodeKind
  = TypeNode { connective: Connective }
  | ComputationNode { behavior: Behavior }


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// EdgeLabel — how a child Node is addressed from its parent.
//
// `Named` (carrying an opaque `Symbol` per K-1 — never a readable string)
// for the labeled-children connectives (Conj/Disj). `Positional` is a
// bare marker for the positional-children connectives (Arrow/Cardinality/
// Instantiation): a positional child's identity IS its 0-based position
// in the parent's `children` list. That list order is the SINGLE
// authority for positional order (CULTURE §8 — canonical, deterministic).
//
// There is deliberately NO stored index. A `Positional { index: Int }`
// field would duplicate the list order — a second authority that can
// disagree (INVARIANTS P2), and it is exactly that duplication that made
// negative / gapped / duplicate / out-of-order indices representable.
// With no index field those illegal states are STRUCTURALLY
// UNREPRESENTABLE — not "rejected by a checker."
type EdgeLabel
  = Named { name: Symbol }
  | Positional


// Edge — a parent Node's reference to one child Node.
type Edge {
  label: EdgeLabel
  target: Node
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Node — THE one recursive type (A1).
//
// Recursion lives SOLELY in `children`: each Edge's `target` is a Node,
// reached only via the children list. There is no other self-reference —
// no `inhabits: Node` slot, no parent pointer. Cross-declaration
// references are name-reference `Atom`s resolved through the namespace
// (A1), which keeps every Node tree finite and the content-hash fold
// well-defined.
type Node {
  kind: NodeKind
  children: List<Edge>
}


// ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
// Edge-discipline invariant (operator directive D3, 2026-05-15).
//
// A connective FIXES its edge discipline: Conj/Disj address children by
// name (`Named`), Arrow/Cardinality/Instantiation by position
// (`Positional`), `Atom` is terminal with no children. The one illegal
// state left to guard is a label-KIND mismatch — a `Named` edge under a
// positional connective (or the reverse), or any edge under `Atom`.
//
// That mismatch is CHECK-enforced, not type-unrepresentable: making it
// unrepresentable would require connective-specific child collections —
// the per-connective redesign D3 explicitly declined ("keep one
// `children: List<Edge>` list"). `node_well_formed` is therefore the
// structural invariant consumers (parse / normalize) evaluate fail-closed
// (INVARIANTS P3).
//
// This is deliberately NOT justified by the STRUCTURE.md A3 precedent.
// A3 is checked-rather-than-unrepresentable because it guards an
// unpreventable human edit (Trusting-Trust — editing the seed). A
// malformed edge is an in-language VALUE — structurally preventable — so
// INVARIANTS P2 would prefer type-unrepresentability; the only reason it
// stays a check here is the D3 no-redesign call, not an A3-style
// impossibility. (Contrast: the closed `Connective` enum below — a 7th
// connective genuinely IS the A3 case, an edit to this file.)
type EdgeDiscipline
  = NoEdges
  | LabeledEdges
  | PositionalEdges


// The discipline a connective fixes for its children.
fn connective_edge_discipline(c: Connective) -> EdgeDiscipline {
  match c {
    Atom { identity: _ } => NoEdges
    Conj => LabeledEdges
    Disj => LabeledEdges
    Arrow => PositionalEdges
    Cardinality => PositionalEdges
    Instantiation => PositionalEdges
  }
}


// Is one edge addressed by name (`Named`) rather than by position?
fn edge_is_named(e: Edge) -> Bool {
  match e.label {
    Named { name: _ } => true
    Positional => false
  }
}

// Is one edge a positional edge? (Its position is its index in the
// parent's `children` list — there is nothing else to check.)
fn edge_is_positional(e: Edge) -> Bool {
  match e.label {
    Named { name: _ } => false
    Positional => true
  }
}

fn all_edges_named(children: List<Edge>) -> Bool {
  fold(children, init: true, f: fn(acc, e) {
    acc && edge_is_named(e: e)
  })
}

fn all_edges_positional(children: List<Edge>) -> Bool {
  fold(children, init: true, f: fn(acc, e) {
    acc && edge_is_positional(e: e)
  })
}

// How many of `children` carry the Named key `name`?
// (`Symbol` equality is the one operation K-1 grants the opaque type.)
fn name_occurrences(name: Symbol, children: List<Edge>) -> Int {
  fold(children, init: 0, f: fn(acc, e) {
    match e.label {
      Named { name: n } => if n == name { acc + 1 } else { acc }
      Positional => acc
    }
  })
}

// Are all Named keys distinct? A `Named` edge is an ADDRESS — two
// children sharing a key make "address a child by name" ambiguous, so a
// labeled connective with a duplicate key is malformed. Checked here so
// downstream consumers can treat a well-formed Conj/Disj's names as keys
// without each re-verifying uniqueness (INVARIANTS P2 — one authority).
fn all_names_distinct(children: List<Edge>) -> Bool {
  fold(children, init: true, f: fn(acc, e) {
    match e.label {
      Named { name: n } => acc && (name_occurrences(name: n, children: children) == 1)
      Positional => acc
    }
  })
}


// Do all of a node's edges satisfy the connective's discipline?
//   NoEdges          → the children list must be empty
//   LabeledEdges     → every edge is addressed by name AND all names are
//                      distinct (a name is an address — no duplicates)
//   PositionalEdges  → every edge is positional (order = list order)
fn edges_conform(children: List<Edge>, d: EdgeDiscipline) -> Bool {
  match d {
    NoEdges => count(children) == 0
    LabeledEdges =>
      all_edges_named(children: children) && all_names_distinct(children: children)
    PositionalEdges => all_edges_positional(children: children)
  }
}


// The LOCAL edge discipline of a single node (its own `children` only).
// A type node's connective fixes the discipline; a computation node has
// no local edge constraint at this substrate-root layer (see below).
fn node_locally_well_formed(n: Node) -> Bool {
  match n.kind {
    TypeNode { connective: c } =>
      edges_conform(children: n.children, d: connective_edge_discipline(c: c))
    ComputationNode { behavior: _ } => true
  }
}

// node_well_formed — the checked structural invariant (D3), WHOLE-TREE.
//
// A node is well-formed iff its own local edge discipline holds AND every
// child subtree is well-formed. This is ONE self-recursive function (not
// a mutual pair) so the v2 seed's termination analyzer accepts it — it
// recurses through `children` exactly the way v2's own recursive Node
// walks do (e.g. `check_bare_containers` in `src/v2/03_normalize.dag`).
// Termination is the A2 implicit case: each `e.target` is a proper
// sub-Node, structurally smaller, so the descent is finite.
//
// One call to `node_well_formed(root)` means the ENTIRE Node tree is
// well-formed — consumers (parse / normalize) do not traverse and
// re-check; that is the single-authority promise (INVARIANTS P2/P3).
//
// NAMED OBLIGATION ON T-6+ (not optional): the computation-node arm of
// `node_locally_well_formed` is an explicitly EMPTY local constraint —
// NOT "computation nodes are unconstrained, forever". The pipeline-stage
// tasks that build the computation substrate (T-6 tokenize … T-22 eval)
// MUST extend that arm with each behavior's edge discipline as they
// land. The whole-tree recursion here is already in force regardless.
fn node_well_formed(n: Node) -> Bool {
  node_locally_well_formed(n: n)
    && fold(n.children, init: true, f: fn(acc, e) {
         acc && node_well_formed(n: e.target)
       })
}
